WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:07.540
We all have questions
that keep us up at night.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.820
Here are two of mine.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.660
The first is: What makes us human?

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:17.460
In a world where
human beings and technology

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.220
are increasingly converging

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.860
and where with the power
of virtual reality

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.980
we can embody anything we want,

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.380
what is it that continues
to keep us human?

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:32.220
Now there's loads
of different answers to this,

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:33.980
but the social anthropologist in me

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:37.180
hangs on to the human power
of forethought.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:41.820
That's our ability to predict
and prepare for what might happen.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.260
Of all the things
that make us uniquely human,

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.060
forethought has got to be
one of the most interesting

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.940
because it enables us
to imagine and create our futures.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:56.060
It gives us the ability
to be deeply creative.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.340
My next question is:
Are we living in reality?

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.420
I'm not channeling my inner Morpheus here.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:06.380
This is honestly something
I think about a lot.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:10.300
I think about how we’re moving towards,
or perhaps have always been,

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.660
living in multiple levels of existence.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.620
After all, simulations aside,

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.140
reality is really just
the stories we tell ourselves

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:21.860
about what we think we see around us

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.860
and may be influenced
by other people’s stories.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:28.340
And so every story that you
have ever heard or ever will,

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.980
including the one
I'm about to tell you now,

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.340
will influence your version of reality.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:37.260
When I was five years old,

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:41.180
I was obsessed with "Alice's Adventures
in Wonderland" by Lewis Carroll.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.500
I loved how impossible it was,
and yet real at the same time.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.380
Back then, this was my virtual reality.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:52.980
And there's an exchange in the book
between Alice and the White Queen,

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:56.140
where the queen is saying
that if you try hard enough,

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:57.660
you can believe in anything,

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:59.100
whether it's possible or not.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:04.860
So now, even though I consider myself
a pretty pragmatic person,

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:09.260
I too try to believe
in six impossible things before breakfast.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:14.340
And with the power of virtual reality, VR,
I can often make them a reality.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:18.940
But as you get older,
your imagination takes a bit of a knock

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.420
and you lose that childlike wonder.

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.700
There's bills to pay
and kids to keep alive

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.300
and work to be done.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.380
And then there's
the monotony of boardrooms

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.820
and PowerPoint presentations

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.060
and video calls.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.620
Hmm.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.100
Now, I've been lucky to work remotely
for a lot of my career,

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.540
and it is amazing
for so many different reasons.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:45.940
Without all of the technology
we have today,

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:48.220
remote work would be impossible.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:51.700
But our overreliance on video calls

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:54.420
is hurting our ability
to be creative as a team.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:56.540
And this is a big problem

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.380
because creativity
isn't just for artists and musicians.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.260
It's what drives innovation
in our workplaces.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:07.500
And without it, we can't solve
the many problems we face as humanity.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:12.980
Now you may have been told
to turn your camera on during video calls

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:16.060
so that people can
more easily connect with you.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.300
This makes a lot of sense
on the surface level

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:21.180
because nobody likes
speaking to a blank screen.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:24.540
But research done
by Carnegie Mellon University

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:27.780
has found that turning our cameras on
during video calls

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.340
impacts our ability
to do creative teamwork.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.020
Now if you're anything
like me on a video call,

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:36.300
your attention is very divided.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:39.180
You're trying to look
at people's facial expressions,

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:41.740
usually, which you can't quite make out.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:44.100
You're trying to listen
to what people are saying,

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:45.860
but you're also looking at yourself

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:48.140
and making sure
you've got no food on your face.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.180
And it just doesn't feel
like a very natural way to communicate.

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:52.620
And it's not.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.060
For millennia, our human ancestors,

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:59.180
who have evolved to be creative
just in order to survive,

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.780
have done so in 3D environments.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:04.180
That's just how our brains are wired.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:06.180
It's only been relatively recently

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:09.740
that we've been distracted
by these glowing 2D screens.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.340
And these screens
are designed to display things.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:15.060
It's called display
technology for a reason.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:17.700
We were never meant
to communicate through them.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:23.740
So if video calls are confusing

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:26.260
and they hurt our creativity,

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:27.900
then why are we still using them?

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:32.980
And when I asked this question,
the answer I get is usually the same.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:35.380
People say it's the best we have

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:39.540
or it's the closest thing
we can get to real life.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:40.820
So this got me thinking.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:46.580
Are video calls really the best we can do
with all of the technology of today?

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:50.020
And what's so good about real life anyway?

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:55.300
What if, rather than
trying to recreate reality,

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:57.420
we aimed for something better?

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:04.100
Now there's already a multibillion-dollar
industry devoted to just this,

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:06.260
instantly transporting you somewhere else

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:08.620
and enabling you
to be present and creative

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:10.860
with people from around the world.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:13.060
And that is the games industry.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:16.300
Many games are laying the foundation
for the metaverse,

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:19.500
and we've heard quite a lot
about this metaverse recently.

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:25.100
I like to think of it as an evolved 3D
and experiential version of the internet.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:28.660
To give you an example.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:33.020
Brands currently have websites
and social media pages.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:35.460
In the metaverse,
these brands may also have

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.940
experiential immersive spaces

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:41.900
that you can visit and engage directly
with whatever they're selling.

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:46.860
So the metaverse is providing
these new social creative spaces.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:48.460
So if you find yourself worrying

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:50.780
that your child
is playing too much Minecraft

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:53.620
or Roblox or Fortnite,

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:57.100
you can remind yourself
that these are not just games.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:05:59.900
They're hanging out with their friends.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:05.260
They’re practicing important life skills
like empathy and teamwork.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:09.620
And they’re often creating
and innovating in these spaces,

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:13.260
far more, dare I say, than you or I are
on our fourth video call of the day.

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:19.380
Virtual reality, whilst it's not
synonymous with the metaverse,

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:24.180
is one of the most powerful technologies
we can use to access these spaces.

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:28.340
And I believe with the power of VR,

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:31.620
the metaverse will reignite
workplace creativity

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:34.100
and inspire us to achieve the impossible.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:37.060
But let's take a step back here.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:41.340
The three key features of virtual reality

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:45.260
can be said to be immersion,
interaction and imagination.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.780
I like to call them the “three magic I’s.”

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:50.260
And the cool thing
about the three magic I's

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:52.860
is that they're also three key drivers,

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:55.220
I believe, of creative
collaboration as well.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:58.060
So let's look at these
in a bit more detail.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:00.780
The first is immersion.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:02.940
Immersion by this definition

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:06.740
is when your senses are isolated
from your physical environment

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:10.420
and put into an entirely
different virtual environment.

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:12.340
And technology is coming on so much

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:16.420
that we won't just be able
to see, hear and feel things in VR,

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:18.620
but we'll be able to taste
and smell them too.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.140
The “internet of senses” is here,

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:25.340
and it's completely revolutionizing
how we interact with each other

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:27.020
in virtual environments.

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:30.940
Immersion gives us copresence.

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:33.660
That's that feeling
of really being there with others.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:35.660
And our brains don't know the difference

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:38.500
between virtual reality
and reality reality

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:40.100
when it comes to copresence,

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:44.220
so we can be together psychologically,
even when we're apart.

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:47.740
And this enables
social creativity to thrive.

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:50.060
The second magic I is interaction.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:54.540
And interaction is where you can
manipulate things within your environment

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:55.860
that isn't really there.

00:07:57.220 --> 00:07:59.860
So when we design physical products,

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:02.500
we imagine them in 3D,

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:04.540
we produce them in 3D.

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:07.540
And yet traditionally,
we've visualized them on 2D screens.

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:10.500
By using VR instead,

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:14.260
we can do this in real time in 3D

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:16.980
whilst being able to cocreate, discuss

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:19.180
and manipulate these designs
with our teams.

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:22.740
The third magic I is imagination,
and this is a biggie for me.

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:28.860
VR gives us the ability
to do anything and be anywhere,

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:31.660
regardless of whether it exists
in reality or not.

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:35.340
Maybe you want to speak to someone
in a different language

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:38.180
and have that instantly translated
above the avatar's head.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:41.460
Perhaps you want to take your brainstorm
on top of Kilimanjaro

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:43.820
or write your notes in dust on the Moon.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:49.820
And yet, with all of the amazing
possibilities afforded to us by VR,

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:55.540
lots of our virtual environments
still mirror our physical environments.

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:58.140
And there's a psychological
reason for this.

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:00.460
A concept called “structured imagination”

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:04.940
means that we tend to rely on existing
paradigms when we create things.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:07.300
In fact, let's try something quickly.

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:11.220
I want you right now to imagine
an animal that doesn't exist

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:12.940
and be as creative as you can be.

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:16.660
Got something?

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:21.460
So even though I told you
to be as creative as you can be,

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:25.060
structured imagination tells me
that most people will have imagined

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:28.500
an animal with eyes of some form
and legs of some form,

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:31.340
perhaps not dissimilar
to Leggy McEyeface over here.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:32.820
Maybe that's just me.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:36.660
But we do the same things
when we think about our workplaces.

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:41.900
We think of an office as a building
with a roof and chairs and desks.

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.860
But we don't need a roof in the metaverse

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:47.180
because there's no precipitation.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:50.860
We don't need chairs with legs
because the floor isn't really there.

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:53.060
In fact, we don't need chairs at all.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:55.500
Do you think that's air
you're breathing right now?

00:09:56.540 --> 00:10:00.380
And the problem with recreating things
like traditional boardrooms

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:03.780
is that we know
that stark corporate spaces

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:06.060
aren't conducive
to creative collaboration.

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:10.660
So what else do we know
about creative collaboration?

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:13.420
Thankfully, we know quite a lot.

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:15.780
We know that retreats,

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:18.260
breaking away from the day-to-day,

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:20.740
can help induce creative thinking.

00:10:22.180 --> 00:10:24.660
We know that some time alone
for reflection

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:28.060
can help the team be more creative
when they come back together.

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:31.020
We can speak to color psychologists

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:33.900
who will tell you that gray
is not a great color

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:36.020
if you're trying to encourage creativity.

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:37.300
We know that nature,

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:39.700
the unexpected

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:45.260
and even messiness
can help induce divergent thinking.

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:51.780
So as we build our metaverse workplaces,
we can be inspired by our imaginations

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:54.300
but guided by all
of this fantastic research

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:55.820
that's been done before.

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:59.900
Lots of people ask me how far
we are away from a true metaverse,

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:02.860
and I don’t really have
an answer for that.

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:05.420
Or I do, but we’d be here all day.

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:07.140
But I will say two things.

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:11.140
The first thing is that
using immersive technology

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:14.100
is not the future
of workplace communication.

00:11:14.140 --> 00:11:16.020
It's the present.

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:18.140
Organizations, large and small,

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:21.540
are already providing VR headsets
to their employees

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:24.660
the same way that they would
traditionally just provide a laptop,

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:27.460
to help their teams feel closer together,

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:30.780
to encourage trust
and to grow relationships.

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:33.220
Secondly, and importantly,

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:38.540
the impact that the metaverse will have
on various areas of our lives

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:40.100
will be profound.

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:43.020
And as with anything created by humans,

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:45.540
there's a potential for it
to be incredibly positive

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:47.580
or devastatingly negative.

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:52.060
Just as social media,
in the previous iteration of the internet,

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:56.380
gave a voice to the voiceless
and created jobs that didn’t exist before,

00:11:57.620 --> 00:12:01.780
it also laid the foundation
for dangerous monopolies

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:06.140
and for misinformation campaigns
that have destabilized entire democracies.

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:08.660
So ...

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:13.260
We have an incredible
opportunity in front of us,

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:18.220
an opportunity to completely
reimagine work and how we collaborate.

00:12:19.220 --> 00:12:24.100
But we must use
our human power of forethought

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:26.580
to create the kind of future
we want to see.

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:32.380
We must become conscious and deliberate
creators of our own destinies.

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:37.620
“Alice in Wonderland”
symbolized curiosity,

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:40.940
imagination and experiencing
the impossible.

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:43.820
VR allows us to do the same.

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:48.980
So here's my ask of every leader today.

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:52.740
Let's put on our VR headsets
and follow that white rabbit.

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:56.540
Let's chase our curiosity
to impossible places in the metaverse

00:12:56.580 --> 00:12:58.700
where anything can happen --

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:02.700
and where we can solve humanity's
biggest problems together.

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:05.020
Thank you.

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:06.220
(Applause)

